WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.340
As Julius Robert Oppenheimer witnessed&nbsp;
the successful detonation of the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.120
first nuclear weapon, he was&nbsp;
haunted by its implications.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.780
I remembered the line from the Hindu&nbsp;
scripture, the Bhagavad Gita…Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:24.060
I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds. I&nbsp;
suppose we all thought that. One way or another.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.520
Oppenheimer was a man of many talents.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.960
He spoke eight languages.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.340
Wrote poetry.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.720
Yet he will forever be remembered&nbsp;
as the father of the atomic bomb.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.720
The man who gave people the power to destroy&nbsp;
themselves was haunted by his own creation.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:47.100
When people first met J. Robert Oppenheimer, what&nbsp;
was immediately obvious to them was his intellect.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.140
A former colleague once said: "The man&nbsp;
was unbelievable! He always gave you the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.540
right answer before you formulated the question."

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.580
Knowledge came easy to Oppenheimer.&nbsp;
He learned Dutch in six weeks just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.340
so he could give a lecture while&nbsp;
on a visit to the Netherlands.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.060
He was born in New York on April 22, 1904,&nbsp;
the older son of German Jewish immigrants.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.320
His father, Julius, was a textile&nbsp;
importer who became very wealthy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.280
His mother was a painter whose family&nbsp;
had been in New York for generations.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.160
He was raised in Manhattan on the upper west side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.740
in an apartment adorned with paintings&nbsp;
by famous artists, including Van Gogh.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.020
After attending an elite private school in&nbsp;
New York City, he went on to Harvard in 1922,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.500
intending to become a chemist&nbsp;
but leaving with an appetite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.360
for physics. Ethical Culture Society School

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.280
He had been attracted to experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.640
physics after taking a course&nbsp;
on thermodynamics at Harvard.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.480
He also studied philosophy, Greek, and&nbsp;
French literature. Ethical Culture School

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.700
He spent his days living in the library,&nbsp;
raiding the place intellectually, as he put it.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.480
He enrolled at Harvard a year late&nbsp;
due to suffering from colitis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.060
an inflammatory bowel disease, but he made up for&nbsp;
it by graduating in three years instead of four.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.120
He then crossed the pond to&nbsp;
the University of Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.080
to conduct research at the Cavendish&nbsp;
Laboratory for experimental physics.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.920
However, he was clumsy in the lab and realized his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.420
forte was not in experimental work&nbsp;
but rather in theoretical physics.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.380
The stress of grad work threw him into&nbsp;
depression and he became emotionally unstable.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.800
He even confessed to lacing an apple with harmful&nbsp;
chemicals in an attempt to poison his tutor with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.540
whom he didn’t get along. Fortunately, the&nbsp;
tutor didn’t eat the apple. (Patrick Blackett)

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.980
Oppenheimer left Cambridge to continue his&nbsp;
studies at one of the centers of theoretical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.940
physics in Europe, the University of&nbsp;
Göttingen (Goat-ting-in) in Germany.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.900
He produced good work and held his&nbsp;
own despite his youth. He was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.480
23 years old when he received his Ph.D.   
 
He subsequently returned to the U.S. where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.580
worked as a research fellow at Harvard and then&nbsp;
the California Institute of Technology, Caltech.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.600
He was so absorbed in his studies&nbsp;
that he often ignored the real world.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.340
But the rise of fascism in Europe&nbsp;
in the 1930s caught his attention.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.660
Oppenheimer agreed with Einstein&nbsp;
that German scientists could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.280
make a nuclear weapon and when they&nbsp;
did, Hitler was prepared to use it.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.500
America watched fearfully as the&nbsp;
Nazis invaded Poland in 1939.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.180
In 1942, Oppenheimer was selected&nbsp;
to direct the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.620
a top-secret U.S. Army project&nbsp;
to develop the atomic bomb.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.320
He brought together the best minds in physics&nbsp;
and eventually managed more than 3,000 people.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.660
He chose Los Alamos, New Mexico as the&nbsp;
primary production facility because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.560
its natural beauty. He had fallen in&nbsp;
love with the mountains and plateaus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.640
when he traveled there as a teen during his&nbsp;
gap year as he recovered from ill health.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.500
Washington threw ridiculous amounts&nbsp;
of money at the project. An initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.460
budget of $6,000 eventually ballooned&nbsp;
into a whopping $2 billion by 1945!

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.140
Before heading the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.120
Oppenheimer taught physics at both Caltech&nbsp;
and the University of California, Berkeley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.920
where he built the physics program and turned&nbsp;
it into the hub for physics research in America.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.240
He had already been working on&nbsp;
nuclear fission - the powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.060
release of energy caused by&nbsp;
the splitting of an atom.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.100
The best element for splitting is the&nbsp;
heaviest element found in nature, uranium.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:32.220
A uranium atom has 92 protons and 146 neutrons,&nbsp;
giving an atomic mass of 238, or U238.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:38.880
A small portion of uranium that’s mined has&nbsp;
the same 92 protons; however, it only has 143&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:46.440
neutrons, giving us U235. This is highly unstable,&nbsp;
which makes it highly splitable or fissionable.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:54.060
When it’s slammed by a neutron, it becomes U236.&nbsp;
It’s so unstable that it splits into two atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.700
krypton and barium. 
 
In the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.460
three more neutrons are released. They&nbsp;
fly out and slam into more U-235 atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.540
causing a chain reaction and&nbsp;
ultimately a huge explosion.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.920
Kind of like when you throw a bowling ball&nbsp;
and the pins jump and strike other pins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.120
and those pins smash into other pins&nbsp;
- all of the energy is released.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:21.000
Research also showed that plutonium 239&nbsp;
would be highly fissionable. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.660
it’s not a naturally occurring&nbsp;
element and had to be manufactured.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.280
Reactors were built in southeastern&nbsp;
Washington state to produce the plutonium.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:30.660
And then….

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.460
On July 16, 1945, scientists detonated a plutonium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.520
bomb over the small town&nbsp;
of Alamagordo, New Mexico.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.980
We knew the world would not be the same. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:52.080
few people laughed. A few people&nbsp;
cried. Most people were silent.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.760
Never before had humanity possessed a weapon&nbsp;
that posed a threat to global civilization.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:05.460
The test's success meant an atomic bomb&nbsp;
was ready to be used by the U.S. military.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.220
The following month, the U.S. military&nbsp;
dropped two atomic bombs on Japan.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:17.460
On August 6, 1945, the most powerful&nbsp;
weapon in the world was dropped on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:24.000
Japanese city of Hiroshima. 140-thousand&nbsp;
people were killed, many vaporized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.920
Thousands more would die in pain in the months&nbsp;
and years that followed from radiation poisoning.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.940
Three days later, another bomb fell on Nagasaki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.140
killing 74,000 people with&nbsp;
equally devastating effects.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:47.040
Japanese Emperor Hirohito decried the&nbsp;
devastating power of “a new and most cruel bomb.”

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.540
Japan surrendered six days later,&nbsp;
abruptly ending World War Two.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.800
Oppenheimer initially expressed&nbsp;
guilt over his creation.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:01.920
He said the weapon had "dramatized so mercilessly&nbsp;
the inhumanity and evil of modern war." 
 &nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:09.480
He continued: "In some sort of crude sense which&nbsp;
no vulgarity, no humor, no overstatements can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:15.300
quite extinguish, the physicists have known sin;&nbsp;
and this is a knowledge which they cannot lose."

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.260
Yet a decade later, he appeared to distance&nbsp;
himself from personal responsibility, pinning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:28.680
it on the state, saying: "I carry no weight on my&nbsp;
conscience. Our work has changed the conditions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:34.560
which men live, but the use made of these changes&nbsp;
is the problem of governments, not of scientists."

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.060
After the war, he became a key&nbsp;
advisor on U.S. atomic policy.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.080
He headed the principal advisory committee&nbsp;
of the successor to the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.180
the Atomic Energy Commission.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:48.960
The Commission was a civilian&nbsp;
agency in control of nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.300
research and the development of nuclear weapons.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.980
He even had a desk in the President's Executive&nbsp;
Offices across the street from the White House.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.540
But then…things began to turn against Oppenheimer.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.800
In December 1953, President&nbsp;
Dwight Eisenhower ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.020
that a "blank wall be placed between&nbsp;
Dr. Oppenheimer and any secret data.”

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.340
Oppenheimer was suspected&nbsp;
of being a communist spy.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.000
He argued he was never interested&nbsp;
in politics and economics.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.460
He apparently wasn’t even aware of the stock&nbsp;
market crash of 1929 until after the fact.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:28.200
However, he started connecting with&nbsp;
left-leaning groups in the thirties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.080
and forties after falling for Jean Tatlock.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.060
In 1936, friends introduced&nbsp;
Oppenheimer to the grad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.400
student who was studying psychology at Stanford.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.240
Tatlock was extremely involved&nbsp;
with politics. She was a member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:46.080
of the Communist Party of America and&nbsp;
supported many left-leaning causes.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.820
Oppenheimer inherited hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of dollars after his father’s death in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.620
thirties and began supporting left-wing efforts&nbsp;
such as the Loyalists in the Spanish Civil War.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.880
They had a passionate relationship&nbsp;
and were together for several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.360
coming close to marriage, but ultimately broke up.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.280
He went on to marry Kitty Puening&nbsp;
in 1940. She was a Berkeley student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.300
and was at one time a Communist Party&nbsp;
member. They had two children together.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.120
He still continued to see his former love and&nbsp;
spent a night with Tatlock while working on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:24.300
secret bomb project in 1943. Tatlock told him&nbsp;
she still loved him. He never saw her again. 
&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.360
She was a complicated person and is said&nbsp;
to have struggled with her sexuality. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:35.340
also suffered from intense depression&nbsp;
and would take her own life in 1944.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:41.340
Perhaps it was Tatlock’s love of the English poet&nbsp;
John Donne that compelled Oppenheimer to name the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:47.100
first test of the atomic bomb “Trinity”, inspired&nbsp;
by Donne’s sonnet about a three-personed God.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.340
Although Oppenheimer’s communist affiliations&nbsp;
were clearly known - they didn’t stop him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:58.980
getting hired on to lead the Manhattan Project.
The FBI saw no reason to revoke his security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.240
clearance when they conducted&nbsp;
an investigation in 1944.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.320
However, the onset of the Cold&nbsp;
War brought renewed scrutiny.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.420
The Soviet Union conducted its first&nbsp;
successful nuclear test in 1949,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.100
ahead of America’s estimates.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.940
People within the government&nbsp;
were suspicious of Oppenheimer.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:23.400
William Liscum Borden, executive director of&nbsp;
the United States Congress Joint Committee on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.480
Atomic Energy, had sent a letter&nbsp;
to FBI director J. Edgar Hoover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:33.360
concluding “more probably than not J. Robert&nbsp;
Oppenheimer is an agent of the Soviet Union.”

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.140
He didn’t back up his claim&nbsp;
with any solid evidence.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:41.220
Borden was close to the chairman of&nbsp;
the Atomic Energy Commission Lewis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.620
Strauss who hated Oppenheimer. The two&nbsp;
butted heads and Strauss never forgot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.580
when Oppenheimer humiliated him at a&nbsp;
Congressional hearing years earlier.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:55.140
In 1954, Oppenheimer was called before a tribunal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.680
of the Atomic Energy Commission to&nbsp;
explain his communist affiliations.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.980
It was a closed-door hearing&nbsp;
that lasted four weeks.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:07.260
Oppenheimer maintained that he had not been&nbsp;
radicalized by his ex-partner, and said his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:12.540
left-wing friends and associations simply gave&nbsp;
him a sense of companionship – nothing more.&nbsp;

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:15.420
He had never been a Communist&nbsp;
party member himself.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.840
Three board members heard his case.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:24.000
By a vote of two to one, they stripped&nbsp;
him of his security clearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:29.100
The guy who delivered the verdict was&nbsp;
none other than Strauss, who hated him.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.560
Oppenheimer was humiliated.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.460
The once powerful scientist lost all his power.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:40.980
The U.S. government no longer trusted the&nbsp;
U.S. government’s top atomic physicist. 
 &nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.860
His scientific peers were outraged.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:48.420
Einstein quipped that the “AEC” in&nbsp;
Atomic Energy Commission actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.360
stood for “Atomic Extermination Conspiracy”

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.980
Wernher von Braun, the chief architect&nbsp;
of the Saturn V that got us to the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.600
declared: “In England, Oppenheimer&nbsp;
would have been knighted.” By the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.220
I did a story on von Braun’s past as a Nazi&nbsp;
scientist which I’ll link in the description.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:09.660
But at least one scientist&nbsp;
was pleased with the outcome.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:15.480
Hungarian physicist Edward Teller resented&nbsp;
the fact that Oppenheimer resisted working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.680
on the hydrogen bomb - which is the more&nbsp;
advanced version of an atomic bomb.
 &nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.140
When President Truman approached the Commission&nbsp;
in 1949 about building a hydrogen bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:28.260
Oppenheimer opposed it, to the anger of Teller.

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:33.060
The US pressed ahead with developing a&nbsp;
thermonuclear weapon anyway and tested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:38.280
it in 1952 on the Marshall Islands.&nbsp;
The H-bomb became Teller’s baby.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.160
During the Oppenheimer hearing, Teller testified:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:46.380
“I would feel personally more secure if&nbsp;
public matters would rest in other hands.”

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:51.780
For his testimony, Teller was shunned by&nbsp;
the scientific community for many years.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.620
Today, hundreds of declassified pages from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.920
secret hearings have revealed&nbsp;
Oppenheimer was not disloyal.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:03.960
The U.S. Department of Energy concludes&nbsp;
“...it proved impossible to link his lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.280
of enthusiasm for the hydrogen bomb&nbsp;
with suspicions of his disloyalty.”

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.480
Producing another weapon with the&nbsp;
potential to be a thousand times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.280
more powerful than the atomic bomb&nbsp;
was unfathomable to Oppenheimer.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:22.740
He felt the hydrogen bomb was unnecessary and&nbsp;
immoral, fearing its use against civilians.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:27.720
He had used his position on the Atomic Energy&nbsp;
Commission to lobby for international control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:31.740
of nuclear weapons to avoid a nuclear&nbsp;
arms race with the Soviet Union. 
 &nbsp;

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:37.320
With his security clearance revoked, he&nbsp;
settled in Princeton, New Jersey where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:42.000
he still ran the Institute for Advanced Study,&nbsp;
a research facility for postdoctoral fellows.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.380
He lived quietly until President John&nbsp;
F. Kennedy invited him to the White&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:52.560
House dinner of Nobel Prize winners in 1962.&nbsp;
Oppenheimer never won a Nobel prize himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:56.100
though he was nominated for the&nbsp;
prize in Physics three times.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.860
When the Kennedy administration offered him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:03.420
a new trial to potentially get his&nbsp;
security clearance back, he declined.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.840
Further evidence that the government&nbsp;
was warming up to him came in 1963 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.720
President Johnson awarded him&nbsp;
one of the highest scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:18.300
honors bestowed by a president, the Fermi&nbsp;
Award, which comes with $50,000 tax-free.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:23.880
In response, Oppenheimer said: "I think&nbsp;
it is just possible, Mr. President,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.860
that it has taken some charity and some&nbsp;
courage for you to make this award today."

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:35.940
Two years later, in 1965, Oppenheimer&nbsp;
was diagnosed with throat cancer. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:38.400
had been a notorious chain smoker all his life.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:40.680
The New York Times once described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:45.180
him “as thin as the wisps from his&nbsp;
chain-smoked cigarettes or pipes”.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:53.700
On February 18, 1967, he died at his&nbsp;
home in Princeton. He was 62 years old.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.260
His wife took his ashes to the island of&nbsp;
Saint John in the U.S. Virgin Islands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.800
where he lived for several months&nbsp;
of the year, and spread his ashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:09.540
in the sea outside of their home, in an&nbsp;
area known today as “Oppenheimer Beach.”

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:17.760
The twentieth century marked a new age in which&nbsp;
the very existence of humankind was threatened.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.660
That threat is more real than ever today with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.780
fears that Russia could use&nbsp;
nuclear weapons in Ukraine.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:28.260
North Korea’s missile launches in October were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:32.340
simulation to “wipe out” South Korean&nbsp;
targets, according to state media.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:37.200
The Doomsday Clock warns we are&nbsp;
getting closer to destroying our world.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:41.220
At the beginning of the Cold War,&nbsp;
we were 7 minutes to midnight.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:44.820
Today, we’re 100 seconds to midnight.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:52.740
If you’ve been inspired by Oppenheimer’s&nbsp;
